\title{Notes on Rubik's abstract polytopes}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{frap}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-09}

\p{What makes a puzzle a Rubik puzzle? A solid shape is only part of the
answer. We also need objects that can change position or orientation, a
collection of allowed moves, and a criterion for being solved. Together
these determine which configurations can be reached and which sequences of
moves undo a scramble. These notes study that structure, beginning with
Rubik puzzles on abstract regular polytopes and proceeding to
#{N\times N\times N} cubes, the #{3\times3\times3} cube, its solution
methods, and play.}

\p{Marchetti's \em{Rubik's Abstract Polytopes} supplies the starting
construction: incidence data and suitable facet rotations define a puzzle
beyond the familiar cube \citek{marchetti2025rubik}. At this level we can
ask which features belong to the shape, which belong to the moves, and
which survive a change of example. Layered cubes then add distinctions
between kinds of pieces and turns. The #{3\times3\times3} cube lets us
follow those distinctions all the way to a practical solve.}

\p{Group theory enters through these questions. Moves compose and can be
undone; they act on puzzle data. Orbits tell us where a piece or a state can
go, while invariants can rule out proposed destinations. Subgroups describe
restrictions such as preserving already solved pieces. Move words turn
these structures into procedures, and a choice of move cost makes their
efficiency a mathematical question. This introduction explains how the
topics connect; detailed definitions, proofs, and worked solves will be
developed in subsequent notes.}

\subtree{
  \title{From incidence to moves}

  \p{An abstract polytope begins with faces and their incidence relations,
  rather than lengths, angles, or a particular embedding. Flags organize
  incident faces of successive ranks, and regularity expresses symmetry
  through an action on flags. Schulte's account of regular incidence
  complexes gives a broader setting for these notions
  \citet{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}. Small polygons and familiar
  polyhedra will provide examples while the definitions remain general.}

  \p{To obtain a Rubik puzzle, the underlying polytope is not enough: we
  must say what is moved and how a facet rotation acts on it. Marchetti's
  construction makes those choices using incidence data
  \citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}. The symmetry group of the
  underlying polytope and the group generated by puzzle moves play
  different roles. Keeping their actions explicit is essential when
  comparing puzzles with different shapes.}

  \p{The first questions are therefore concrete even at this general
  level: what does one move change, what does it fix, and what happens when
  two moves are composed? A worked facet action will make the construction
  visible before broader structural results are considered. The purpose
  of the abstraction is to make such comparisons possible, without
  requiring a separate theory for every picture of a puzzle.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The family of layered cubes}

  \p{For an #{N\times N\times N} cube, changing #{N} enriches the piece
  structure while the surrounding cube remains three-dimensional.
  Abstract dimension and physical layer count are different parameters.
  Passing to this family requires its own explicit description of pieces,
  allowed layer turns, and the information recorded in a configuration.}

  \p{Comparing #{N=2}, #{N=3}, and #{N=4} brings the new questions into
  view: which piece types occur, which positions communicate under moves,
  and which distinctions depend on odd or even layer count? Labels and
  colors also matter. Two individually labelled pieces can be distinct in
  a mathematical model while looking identical to a player. The notes
  will state whether whole-cube rotations change the recorded state or
  are treated as an equivalence.}

  \p{Bonzio, Loi, and Peruzzi study the general cube and its configuration
  constraints \citek{bonzio2018nxnxn}; Salkinder connects the general family
  with questions about solution length \citek{salkinder2021nxnxn}. These
  sources will be compared with their conventions intact. Permutation
  actions and orientation data explain the piece structure; invariants
  constrain reachability. A necessary constraint is not yet a proof that
  every configuration satisfying it can be reached.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The three-layer cube in detail}

  \p{The #{3\times3\times3} cube is the place to make the description
  fully explicit: corner and edge positions, their orientations, face
  moves, and the chosen frame of reference. A move should be readable as
  an action on those data as well as recognizable as a turn of the puzzle.
  Orientation conventions need enough worked examples that a reader can
  compute a move without guessing the meaning of a coordinate.}

  \p{Three questions must be distinguished. Can a proposed arrangement be
  described in the chosen coordinates? Does it satisfy the constraints
  preserved by legal moves? Can it actually be reached from the solved
  state? A characterization of reachability must justify the last step as
  well as the necessary constraints. This is where permutation parity,
  orientation constraints, and constructive move sequences meet.}

  \p{A description of the reachable states still leaves a practical
  problem: given one of them, how do we choose moves that solve it? The
  transition from characterizing states to constructing a solution leads
  naturally to the study of methods.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Why solution methods work}

  \p{A solving method organizes a sequence of smaller goals. At each
  stage, some information has been fixed and some remains free. The useful
  mathematical question is which moves accomplish the next goal while
  preserving what has already been achieved. Stabilizers and subgroup
  chains give language to that requirement. Conjugation relocates the
  effect of a known sequence; commutators can help isolate effects, whose
  actual support must be checked in each example.}

  \p{Explaining why a method works and judging how well it serves a player
  are related but different tasks. A complete method needs an argument
  that its stages cover the remaining cases and terminate at a solved
  state. Practical use also depends on recognition, memorization, and ease
  of execution. Computational search raises further questions about time,
  memory, and solution length.}

  \p{Cayley graphs describe move sequences as paths between group
  elements; actions on partial information lead to related Schreier-graph
  viewpoints. Such descriptions become useful when comparing reduction
  strategies or searching for shorter sequences. Any length comparison
  must specify the allowed moves and their costs. The goal is to connect
  a method's observable effect with its mathematical explanation.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{From move sequences to play}

  \p{Play gives the mathematics an immediate test of intelligibility.
  A notation should identify a move unambiguously; a displayed turn should
  agree with the stated action; and replaying a sequence should reproduce
  the same state. Scrambling, undoing, recognizing a case, and following a
  solution all depend on those agreements.}

  \p{Small interactive examples can accompany the earlier mathematics:
  follow a piece through a turn, compare two orders of moves, or inspect
  what a sequence preserves. A fuller playing interface can then bring
  together notation, visualization, replay, and methods. An animation
  illustrates a claim; a proof must explain why it holds for the stated
  class of moves and states.}

  \p{The existing [Rubik's Cube resources](uts-016J) collect material on
  notation, methods, and demonstrations. The companion
  [FRAP repository](https://github.com/utensil/frap) will support later
  formal and executable work. The present notes begin the mathematical
  exposition; they do not yet provide a formalized puzzle model or solver.}
}
